  "independent_source_review": "# Independent source review\n\n2026-10-25, read-only; no build, GPU run, trace or performance endorsement.\nReviewed source.patch SHA256 124f905cc15a3f5b45d9b6bed42e19a71c44539d9b1f5ac35065a29880db3ae9 and the three private source files.\n\nNo must-fix correctness/lifetime defect found in this bounded change; suitable to merge as cold native capture-allocation reuse, not as a demonstrated throughput improvement.\n\n- `kDiscard` remains the generic invalidation entry. Configuration mutation, Queue-owner replacement, native import/owner reset and failed capture paths still discard both saved storage and variants. Commands mode still resets the cache. The patch does not turn these into native-argument reuse.\n- `kNativeArguments` is reached through RegionAccess::invalidate_commands after resource_control_ready: running/failed/preparing invocations, retained results and unavailable completion observation are rejected. It removes active captured ranges, launch spans, qualification and all variant keys. It keeps at most one backend-authorized CaptureRebuildStorage for each original immutable range, never the old launchable artifact.\n- `kKeepExistingSeed` is limited to stable ordinary TensorView carriers with neither previous nor current native inputs after revision invalidation. It cannot manufacture a seed from an active command or skip prepare_bindings/seal. This avoids accidentally hard-clearing an already safe seed during the subsequent binding preparation.\n- The existing Compute contract explicitly permits a retired permanently nonlaunchable allocation to outlive old provider arguments (`detail/command_capture.h:29-34`). CUDA requires unique/nonbusy state before setting rebuild_only; launch rejects rebuild_only. A retained state also retains its original context. No old pointer is used as permission or a restored binding key.\n- CUDA end-capture accepts only same-context unique idle rebuild storage, runs actual cudaGraphExecUpdate against the newly captured graph, and transfers only the executable on success. Only structural update incompatibility falls back to instantiate; other native errors propagate. On failure the ordinary nonlaunchable seed owner is destroyed. There is no optimistic success/replay route.\n- Non-CUDA backends that do not authorize recycle simply destroy the moved artifact under the pre-existing callback contract. No new public vtable/layout, cache variants, provider route or mathematical change was introduced.\n\nThe parent's 6 successful native updates and 6+2 process screen are separate actual evidence. I did not independently reproduce them; the reported ~0.82% overlapping interval is insufficient for a speedup claim.\n",
